window.docContents[166].resolve({"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.Party/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___Party":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames › Party.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof 3 headline theorems depend on it 13 of 58 lines trusted trust banner B3, trusted in part source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nProtocol parties and alternating epoch ownershipTrusted in part. The theorems of Computational/Theorems/ use every definition in this file except Party.senderOf: read them. Party.senderOf and the theorems here are checked by Lean. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.Defines the two participants (Party), partner swap (Party.other), and alternating epoch ownership (Party.sendsIn, Party.senderOf: A sends in odd epochs, B in even epochs) shared by the CKA and SM games (ACD19 §3.2.1, §4.1.1).\n\n\n\ninductive PartyL30–34 ↗#Two-party protocol participant (A or B).\n\n\n\ndef otherL38–41 ↗#Partner of a party (A ↔ B).\n\n4 declarations not shown (lines 43–46): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoGames.Party.other_A, CryptoGames.Party.other_B, CryptoGames.Party.other_other, CryptoGames.Party.other_ne\n\ndef sendsInL48–51 ↗#Whether P sends in epoch t (A in odd epochs, B in even epochs).\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 53–59): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoGames.Party.senderOf\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/Party.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.Party/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___Party"},"/Tier-3___-trusted___-not-read/AArch64-semantics/Arm64.Trusted.Syscalls.Kernel.Signal/#file-Arm64___Trusted___Syscalls___Kernel___Signal":{"contents":"Paper › arm64-semantics/Arm64/Trusted/Syscalls/Kernel › Signal.leanTier 3 · arm64-semantics\n\nTier 3 · arm64-semantics used by the symbolic proof 2 headline theorems depend on it 283 of 338 lines trusted source ↗Trusted, reviewed with the engine. The AArch64 model in arm64-semantics is Tier 3: it is reviewed in its own repository (check-trust-guards). The declarations that the headline statements use are shown for completeness.\n\nDeclarations on this page (28)def SYS_TKILLdef SYS_TGKILLdef SYS_SIGALTSTACKdef SYS_RT_SIGACTIONdef SYS_RT_SIGPROCMASKdef LINUX_SIGABRTdef LINUX_SIGBUSdef LINUX_SIGKILLdef LINUX_SIGSEGVdef LINUX_SIGSTOPdef SIG_DFLdef SIG_IGNdef SIG_UNBLOCKABLE_MASKdef UAPI_SA_FLAGSdef SS_ONSTACKdef SS_DISABLEdef SS_AUTODISARMdef MINSIGSTKSZdef SigAction.toBytesdef SigAltStack.autodisarmdef SigAltStack.onSigStackdef SigAltStack.flagsdef SigAltStack.toBytesdef execRtSigprocmaskdef execSigaltstackdef execRtSigactiondef execTkilldef execTgkill\n\nSignal and Abort SyscallsLinux 7.3 AArch64 signal syscall numbers, ABI constants, SigAction/SigAltStack binary layouts, and execution semantics for rt_sigprocmask (kernel/signal.c:3247), sigaltstack (kernel/signal.c:4515, include/linux/sched/signal.h:571-608), rt_sigaction (kernel/signal.c:4551), tgkill (kernel/signal.c:4024), and tkill (kernel/signal.c:4038).\n\n\n\ndef SYS_TKILLL27–28 ↗#Linux AArch64 tkill syscall number (__NR_tkill = 130, include/uapi/asm-generic/unistd.h:358, kernel/signal.c:4038).\n\ndef SYS_TGKILLL30–31 ↗#Linux AArch64 tgkill syscall number (__NR_tgkill = 131, include/uapi/asm-generic/unistd.h:360, kernel/signal.c:4024).\n\ndef SYS_SIGALTSTACKL33–34 ↗#Linux AArch64 sigaltstack syscall number (__NR_sigaltstack = 132, include/uapi/asm-generic/unistd.h:362, kernel/signal.c:4515).\n\ndef SYS_RT_SIGACTIONL36–37 ↗#Linux AArch64 rt_sigaction syscall number (__NR_rt_sigaction = 134, include/uapi/asm-generic/unistd.h:366, kernel/signal.c:4551).\n\ndef SYS_RT_SIGPROCMASKL39–40 ↗#Linux AArch64 rt_sigprocmask syscall number (__NR_rt_sigprocmask = 135, include/uapi/asm-generic/unistd.h:368, kernel/signal.c:3247).\n\ndef LINUX_SIGABRTL42–43 ↗#POSIX / Linux SIGABRT (SIGIOT) signal number (6, include/uapi/asm-generic/signal.h:20).\n\ndef LINUX_SIGBUSL45–46 ↗#POSIX / Linux SIGBUS signal number (7, include/uapi/asm-generic/signal.h:21).\n\ndef LINUX_SIGKILLL48–49 ↗#POSIX / Linux SIGKILL signal number (9, include/uapi/asm-generic/signal.h:23).\n\ndef LINUX_SIGSEGVL51–52 ↗#POSIX / Linux SIGSEGV signal number (11, include/uapi/asm-generic/signal.h:25).\n\ndef LINUX_SIGSTOPL54–55 ↗#POSIX / Linux SIGSTOP signal number (19, include/uapi/asm-generic/signal.h:33).\n\ndef SIG_DFLL57–58 ↗#Default signal disposition constant (SIG_DFL = 0, include/uapi/asm-generic/signal-defs.h:22).\n\ndef SIG_IGNL60–61 ↗#Ignore signal disposition constant (SIG_IGN = 1, include/uapi/asm-generic/signal-defs.h:23).\n\ndef SIG_UNBLOCKABLE_MASKL63–66 ↗#Bitmask of signals stripped from sa_mask by do_sigaction (sigmask(SIGKILL) | sigmask(SIGSTOP) = (1 << 8) | (1 << 18) = 0x40100, SIG_KERNEL_ONLY_MASK in include/linux/signal.h:403).\n\ndef UAPI_SA_FLAGSL68–75 ↗#AArch64 sa_flags bits kept by do_sigaction (act->sa.sa_flags &= UAPI_SA_FLAGS in kernel/signal.c:4401-4404): UAPI_SA_FLAGS = SA_NOCLDSTOP | SA_NOCLDWAIT | SA_SIGINFO | SA_ONSTACK | SA_RESTART | SA_NODEFER | SA_RESETHAND | SA_EXPOSE_TAGBITS | __ARCH_UAPI_SA_FLAGS (include/linux/signal_types.h:76-86) with __ARCH_UAPI_SA_FLAGS = SA_RESTORER on arm64 (0x04000000, arch/arm64/include/uapi/asm/signal.h:21), i.e. 0x1 | 0x2 | 0x4 | 0x08000000 | 0x10000000 | 0x40000000 | 0x80000000 | 0x800 | 0x04000000 (include/uapi/asm-generic/signal-defs.h:30-65), zero-extended to the 64-bit sa_flags.\n\ndef SS_ONSTACKL77–78 ↗#Alternate signal stack active flag (SS_ONSTACK = 1, include/uapi/linux/signal.h:8).\n\ndef SS_DISABLEL80–81 ↗#Alternate signal stack disabled flag (SS_DISABLE = 2, include/uapi/linux/signal.h:9).\n\ndef SS_AUTODISARML83–84 ↗#Alternate signal stack auto-disarm flag (SS_AUTODISARM = 1U << 31, include/uapi/linux/signal.h:12).\n\ndef MINSIGSTKSZL86–88 ↗#Minimum AArch64 alternate signal stack byte size (MINSIGSTKSZ = 5120, arch/arm64/include/uapi/asm/signal.h:23; note 2048 in asm-generic is 32-bit compat only).\n\ndef SigAction.toBytesL90–93 ↗#Serialize a 32-byte AArch64 struct k_sigaction (handler, flags, restorer, mask) in little-endian byte order (include/linux/signal_types.h).\n\ndef SigAltStack.autodisarmL95–98 ↗#Whether the recorded raw sas_ss_flags word of sas has SS_AUTODISARM set (current->sas_ss_flags & SS_AUTODISARM in include/linux/sched/signal.h:595).\n\ndef SigAltStack.onSigStackL100–106 ↗#True when stack pointer sp lies on the active alternate signal stack sas (on_sig_stack / __on_sig_stack for downward-growing AArch64 stacks in include/linux/sched/signal.h:571-600): returns false when sas.autodisarm is set (SS_AUTODISARM) or sas.size = 0, and otherwise checks sas.sp < sp ∧ sp - sas.sp ≤ sas.size using unsigned 64-bit arithmetic ((sas.sp, sas.sp + sas.size]).\n\ndef SigAltStack.flagsL108–116 ↗#Reported 32-bit ss_flags word for sas at stack pointer sp (sas_ss_flags(sp) | (current->sas_ss_flags & SS_FLAG_BITS) in include/linux/sched/signal.h:602-608 and kernel/signal.c:4468-4469).\n\ndef SigAltStack.toBytesL118–122 ↗#Serialize a 24-byte AArch64 stack_t (ss_sp : u64 at +0, ss_flags : i32 at +8, 4 zero padding bytes at +12 from memset(oss, 0, sizeof(stack_t)), ss_size : u64 at +16; include/uapi/asm-generic/signal.h:85-89, kernel/signal.c:4465-4469).\n\ndef execRtSigprocmaskL124–163 ↗#rt_sigprocmask(how, set, oldset, sigsetsize) (__NR_rt_sigprocmask = 135, kernel/signal.c:3247 SYSCALL_DEFINE4(rt_sigprocmask)): validates sigsetsize == 8 (sizeof(sigset_t) on 64-bit Linux), user-memory readability and non-wrapping 8-byte range of set (when non-null), how ∈ {SIG_BLOCK(0), SIG_UNBLOCK(1), SIG_SETMASK(2)} when set ≠ 0, and writability and non-wrapping 8-byte range of oldset (8 bytes when non-null).* [INTENTIONAL_SCOPE]: Non-zero blocked signal masks are out of scope. Because a process starts with an empty blocked mask (0), set == 0, how == SIG_UNBLOCK(1), and SIG_BLOCK(0) / SIG_SETMASK(2) with (read64 set &&& ~~~SIG_UNBLOCKABLE_MASK) == 0 (SIGKILL / SIGSTOP stripped per sigdelsetmask in kernel/signal.c:3262) all preserve an empty blocked mask (oldset receives 8 zero bytes when non-null), whereas SIG_BLOCK or SIG_SETMASK with any blockable signal bit set fails closed with .Exception.* [OVERPINNED_BEHAVIOR]: Validates sigsetsize -> copy_from_user(set) -> how / empty-mask scope check -> copy_to_user(oldset), matching kernel/signal.c:3254-3281.\n\ndef execSigaltstackL165–222 ↗#sigaltstack(uss, uoss) (__NR_sigaltstack = 132, SYSCALL_DEFINE2(sigaltstack) and do_sigaltstack in kernel/signal.c:4458-4527): models queries and updates of the per-thread 24-byte stack_t alternate signal stack (ss_sp : u64 at +0, 32-bit ss_flags at +8 ignoring 4-byte caller padding at +12, ss_size : u64 at +16):* non-null uss that wraps modulo 2 ^ 64 or is not 24-byte readable returns -EFAULT (copy_from_user(&new, uss, sizeof(stack_t)) before state mutation);* when uss ≠ 0 and s.sigAltStack.onSigStack s.sp (sp ∈ (sas.sp, sas.sp + sas.size] without SS_AUTODISARM), returns -EPERM;* when uss ≠ 0 and (ss_flags &&& ~~~SS_AUTODISARM) ∉ {0, SS_ONSTACK, SS_DISABLE}, returns -EINVAL;* when uss ≠ 0 and (ss_flags &&& ~~~SS_AUTODISARM) == SS_DISABLE, records { sp := 0, size := 0, ssFlags := ss_flags } without checking ss_size (kernel/signal.c:4496-4499); otherwise records { sp := ss_sp, size := ss_size, ssFlags := ss_flags } after requiring ss_size ≥ MINSIGSTKSZ (5120, returning -ENOMEM if smaller) unless the recorded (sas_ss_sp, sas_ss_size, sas_ss_flags) already equals (ss_sp, ss_size, ss_flags): that unchanged-settings early return (kernel/signal.c:4486-4493, after the -EPERM/-EINVAL checks and before SS_DISABLE normalization and the MINSIGSTKSZ check) leaves the state unchanged, which equals the store below (every reachable recorded SS_DISABLE-mode setting already has sp = size = 0);* when uoss ≠ 0, writes the previous 24-byte zero-padded stack_t snapshot (oldBytes, captured before updating s.sigAltStack) to uoss (returning -EFAULT if uoss wraps modulo 2 ^ 64 or is not 24-byte writable, matching copy_to_user(uoss, &old, sizeof(stack_t)) after do_sigaltstack in kernel/signal.c:4521-4525);* [OVERPINNED_BEHAVIOR]: checks copy_from_user(uss) -> do_sigaltstack -> copy_to_user(uoss), matching kernel/signal.c:4519-4526.\n\ndef execRtSigactionL224–285 ↗#rt_sigaction(signum, act, oldact, sigsetsize) (__NR_rt_sigaction = 134, SYSCALL_DEFINE4(rt_sigaction) and do_sigaction in kernel/signal.c:4551): models per-signal SIG_DFL (0) and SIG_IGN (1) disposition queries and updates across the 64 Linux signals (1 ≤ signum ≤ 64, _NSIG = 64), plus recording user-space handlers for the synchronous hardware fault signals SIGBUS (7) and SIGSEGV (11), in State.sigActions without asynchronous signal delivery:* sigsetsize != 8 (sizeof(sigset_t)) returns -EINVAL;* non-null act that wraps modulo 2 ^ 64 or is not 32-byte readable returns -EFAULT (copy_from_user);* signum ≤ 0 or signum > 64 returns -EINVAL (!valid_signal(sig));* non-null act with signum == SIGKILL (9) or signum == SIGSTOP (19) returns -EINVAL (sig_kernel_only(sig));* [INTENTIONAL_SCOPE]: non-null act whose sa_handler is neither SIG_DFL (0) nor SIG_IGN (1) is permitted only when signum == SIGSEGV (11) or signum == SIGBUS (7) (recording the disposition in State.sigActions); all other signals fail closed with .Exception \"unmodeled rt_sigaction\". Hardware faults in Arm64/Trusted/ISA/Eval.lean always raise .Exception directly (never .Signaled or invoking user handlers), and execTkill / execTgkill on SIGSEGV / SIGBUS or non-SIG_DFL/SIG_IGN SIGABRT likewise raise .Exception;* when act ≠ 0, records { handler, flags &&& UAPI_SA_FLAGS, restorer, mask &&& ~~~SIG_UNBLOCKABLE_MASK } in State.sigActions signum (sa_flags &= UAPI_SA_FLAGS and sigdelsetmask in do_sigaction); recorded flags are thus already masked, so oldact matches Linux's oact->sa.sa_flags &= UAPI_SA_FLAGS;* when oldact ≠ 0, writes the previous 32-byte k_sigaction to oldact (returning -EFAULT if oldact wraps modulo 2 ^ 64 or is not 32-byte writable, matching copy_to_user after do_sigaction);* [OVERPINNED_BEHAVIOR]: checks sigsetsize -> copy_from_user(act) -> do_sigaction(signum) -> copy_to_user(oldact), matching kernel/signal.c:4559-4573.\n\ndef execTkillL287–310 ↗#tkill(tid, sig) (__NR_tkill = 130, do_tkill in kernel/signal.c:4038):* if tid ≤ 0, sig < 0, or sig > 64 (_NSIG), returns -EINVAL;* [INTENTIONAL_SCOPE]: if tid == 1 (SIM_TID) and sig == SIGABRT (6):* when SIGABRT's disposition is SIG_DFL (0), terminates the process with fatal signal status .Signaled 6#7 (shell / qemu-aarch64 exit code 128 + 6 = 134);* when SIGABRT's disposition is SIG_IGN (1), drops the ignored signal (sig_ignored in kernel/signal.c) and returns 0;* otherwise fails closed with .Exception \"unmodeled tkill\";* any other (tid, sig) pair fails closed with .Exception \"unmodeled tkill\".\n\ndef execTgkillL312–336 ↗#tgkill(tgid, tid, sig) (__NR_tgkill = 131, do_tkill in kernel/signal.c:4024):* if tid ≤ 0, tgid ≤ 0, sig < 0, or sig > 64 (_NSIG), returns -EINVAL;* [INTENTIONAL_SCOPE]: if tgid == 1, tid == 1 (SIM_TID), and sig == SIGABRT (6):* when SIGABRT's disposition is SIG_DFL (0), terminates the process with fatal signal status .Signaled 6#7 (shell / qemu-aarch64 exit code 128 + 6 = 134);* when SIGABRT's disposition is SIG_IGN (1), drops the ignored signal (sig_ignored in kernel/signal.c) and returns 0;* otherwise fails closed with .Exception \"unmodeled tgkill\";* any other (tgid, tid, sig) triple fails closed with .Exception \"unmodeled tgkill\".\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"arm64-semantics/Arm64/Trusted/Syscalls/Kernel/Signal.lean","id":"/Tier-3___-trusted___-not-read/AArch64-semantics/Arm64.Trusted.Syscalls.Kernel.Signal/#file-Arm64___Trusted___Syscalls___Kernel___Signal"}});